.Dd October 18, 2026
.Dt ADDUSER 8
.Os OxideBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm adduser
.Nd add users interactively
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl f Ar file
.Op Fl G Ar groups
.Op Fl g Ar group
.Op Fl k Ar skel
.Op Fl L Ar class
.Op Fl s Ar shell
.Op Fl w Cm no | none | random | yes
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
script adds users by asking for each account's details: the login
name, full name, user ID (by default the one
.Ql pw usernext
gives), login group, other groups, login class, shell (one named in
.Pa /etc/shells ,
or
.Cm nologin ) ,
home directory and password.
It shows a summary to confirm, then creates the account and its home
directory with
.Xr pw 8 .
It is a simplified
.Fx
.Nm .
It must be run by the superuser.
.Pp
A password typed at the prompts is passed to
.Xr pw 8
on its standard input, never on a command line.
.Pp
The options set the defaults the prompts offer:
.Bl -tag -width "-w method"
.It Fl f Ar file
Add the accounts in
.Ar file
without asking, one per line:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
name:uid:gid:class:change:expire:gecos:home:shell:password
.Ed
.Pp
Empty fields take the defaults; the password is in clear text.
Lines starting with
.Ql #
are skipped.
.It Fl G Ar groups
Other groups for each user, separated by commas.
.It Fl g Ar group
The login group; by default a new group named like the user.
.It Fl k Ar skel
The skeleton directory for new homes.
.It Fl L Ar class
The login class.
.It Fl s Ar shell
The shell; by default
.Cm sh .
.It Fl w Ar method
How passwords are set:
.Cm yes
(ask; the default),
.Cm no
(none usable),
.Cm none
(empty) or
.Cm random
(generated and shown).
In a
.Fl f
file, an empty password field follows this method,
.Cm yes
then meaning none usable.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
0 on success; 1 if an account could not be added, 64 for a bad
command line, 77 when not run by the superuser.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr passwd 1 ,
.Xr usermgmt.conf 5 ,
.Xr pw 8 ,
.Xr rmuser 8 ,
.Xr useradd 8
.Sh HISTORY
An
.Nm
command appeared in
.Fx 2.0 .
This implementation was written for OxideBSD.
